    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Nature</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Why Is Garajau Named After a Seabird?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>2 January 2027</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Because garajau is the Madeiran and Azorean name for the common tern, a small white seabird that nests on the island&#x27;s cliffs from spring to autumn. The headland east of Funchal, the village above it and the marine reserve below all carry the bird&#x27;s name, though no old document we found records who named it, or when.</p>

      <h2>What exactly is a garajau?</h2><p>A tern. In Madeira and the Azores, garajau means the common tern, Sterna hirundo: a slim seabird, white and pale grey, with a black cap, a red bill tipped with black and a deeply forked tail. In mainland Portugal the same word is used for a different bird, the Sandwich tern, so a Lisbon birdwatcher and a Madeiran fisherman can mean different birds by it.</p><p>Common terns are summer visitors here. They arrive around March, nest in May and June on islets and rocky cliffs, and are gone by October. They breed in small numbers on all of the islands. A rarer cousin, the roseate tern, garajau-rosado, also nests in Madeira in very small numbers between mid April and September. The official management plan for the Garajau reserve lists the common tern among the birds that nest on or visit its cliffs.</p><h2>Is the place really named after the bird?</h2><p>Almost certainly, but nobody wrote it down. A headland called after the birds that wheeled over it fits the way Madeira's coast was named, with places such as Câmara de Lobos named after the monk seals in its bay. Yet we found no early chronicle or document that says terns nested at Ponta do Garajau when the name was given, or who gave it. If you read a confident version with dates and names, treat it with care.</p><p>What is certain is how far the name spread. Ponta do Garajau is the headland. Garajau is also the area of Caniço above it, the beach at the foot of the cliff, and the marine reserve offshore.</p><h2>How do you tell a tern from a gull?</h2><p>It takes a few seconds once you know what to look for. The big pale bird you see everywhere around Funchal harbour is usually the yellow-legged gull, gaivota in Portuguese. A tern is different in five ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Size and shape.</strong> A tern is much slimmer and lighter, with long, narrow, pointed wings.</li><li><strong>The tail.</strong> Forked like a swallow's. Gulls have a square or rounded tail.</li><li><strong>The head.</strong> In summer a common tern wears a neat black cap.</li><li><strong>The bill.</strong> Thin and red with a dark tip, not the heavy yellow bill of a gull.</li><li><strong>The way it feeds.</strong> A tern hovers over the water, then drops head first in a quick, splashing dive. Gulls mostly pick food from the surface.</li></ol><p>The best moments to watch are calm, bright days in early summer, when the parents are feeding chicks and fishing close to the shore. A tern flying with a small silver fish held crosswise in its bill is usually taking it to a chick or a mate.</p><p>The large brown and white bird gliding low on stiff wings is something else again: the cagarra, Cory's shearwater. Our <a href="/posts/birdwatching-in-madeira-seabirds-endemic-birds">guide to seabirds and endemic birds in Madeira</a> covers the rest.</p><h2>Whose is the white statue on the headland?</h2><p>It is the Cristo Rei do Garajau, Christ the King with open arms, inaugurated on 30 October 1927, the same day as the road to it from Cancela. Sources give its height as 14 or 15 metres including the pedestal. It is usually credited to the French sculptor Georges Serraz, some sources adding Pierre Charles Lenoir, and it was paid for by Aires de Ornelas, son of the last morgado, the hereditary landowner, of Caniço. It stands on private land, and in August 2026 the local parish leader called for repairs before its centenary in 2027.</p><p>The headland has a harder past too. In 1942 a whaling station opened at Garajau, before the industry moved to Caniçal. Our article on <a href="/posts/did-madeira-have-whaling-canical">Madeira's whaling years</a> tells that story.</p><h2>What is the Garajau marine reserve?</h2><p>The sea below the headland, protected since 1986. The Garajau Partial Nature Reserve was Madeira's first marine reserve, and Diário de Notícias and Oceanographic Magazine both describe it as the first exclusively marine reserve in Portugal. Its official management plan gives these facts:</p><ul><li>376 hectares of sea, from Ponta do Lazareto, just east of Funchal, to Ponta da Oliveira in Caniço.</li><li>It reaches out to the 50 metre depth line, and never less than 600 metres from the shore.</li><li>Fishing of any kind is banned, and motor boats may not enter, except to land at the beaches.</li><li>Wardens have watched it since 1996 from a post at the Lazareto pier.</li><li>The coast inside holds 19 small pebble beaches and 7 caves, and the water is often clear beyond 20 metres. Its best known residents are the dusky groupers, meros in Portuguese, that divers come to see.</li></ul><p>The reserve was created to protect the life on the sea floor, not the birds. The terns simply share the cliffs.</p><h2>Can you see Garajau from a boat?</h2><p>From a distance, yes. As you leave Marina do Funchal, Ponta do Garajau is the headland on the eastern side of the bay, with the white statue on top. Up close, the reserve rules keep motor boats out. The plan's one allowance is a 35 metre corridor off Praia do Garajau, where pleasure boats may moor between sunrise and sunset to reach the beach. That is why the best ways to know Garajau are from the clifftop, from the beach at its foot, or underwater with a licensed dive centre. Our <a href="/posts/garajau-nature-reserve-madeira-guide">Garajau Nature Reserve guide</a> covers those.</p><p>It depends what you are after. Both Chifbay trips run west, towards Cabo Girão, and the reasons are set out on the <a href="/experiences">Chifbay experiences page</a>. Terns are not confined to Garajau, though. They nest in small numbers around the whole island, so on a summer <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip along the south coast</a> it is worth watching for a slim white bird hovering over the water and folding into a dive. Nobody can promise one, but now you will know its name.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What does garajau mean in English?</h3><p>Tern. In Madeira and the Azores, garajau is the common tern, Sterna hirundo. In mainland Portugal the word is used for the Sandwich tern.</p><h3>When can you see terns in Madeira?</h3><p>Common terns arrive around March and leave by October. They nest in May and June on islets and rocky cliffs.</p><h3>When was the Cristo Rei in Garajau built?</h3><p>It was inaugurated on 30 October 1927, on the headland of Ponta do Garajau, east of Funchal.</p><h3>Can boats enter the Garajau reserve?</h3><p>Motor boats may not enter, except to land at the beaches. Fishing of any kind is banned inside the reserve.</p>

      <blockquote>The statue faces the sea, the reserve guards the floor, and the tern that named them both only stays for the summer.</blockquote>
    </article>
